          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong Markov kernel
            (also called
            = succeed "," do
              %strong transition kernel
            = succeed "," do
              %strong stochastic kernel
            = succeed ")" do
              %strong probability kernel
            %em transition kernel
            denotes a more general, non-normalized mapping.)
          %p
            It can be thought of as a generalization of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stochastic+map"} stochastic map
            outside the finite discrete case. (Sometimes the term “stochastic map” is itself used to denote a Markov kernel.)
          %p
            Markov kernels, in the form of regular conditional distributions (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#regular_conditional_distributions"} below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conditional+probability"} conditional probability
            outside of the discrete case (and without incurring in paradoxes).
          %p
            Markov kernels and their categories are among the basic building blocks of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+probability"} categorical probability
